      <h2 style="margin-bottom:8px">What AI SEO Actually Means for {f.LFTITLE}</h2>

      <h3>{f.LFH1}</h3>
      <p>{f.LFU}</p>
      <p>{f.LFD}</p>
      <p>If your name isn't there, the {f.JOBWORD} never enters your pipeline. Unlike a lost bid, you never even learn it existed. That's the exposure: {f.LFEXPOSE}</p>

      <h3>Being recommended is earned the same way trust always was</h3>
      <p>AI engines don't invent local {f.PROS}. They assemble answers from what the web already says about you — review volume and recency, {f.CREDS}, a complete and active Google Business Profile, consistent listings across directories, mentions on sites that carry weight, and pages that genuinely cover the work you do in the places you do it.</p>
      <p>That's good news for a well-run {f.BIZ}. You don't need to game anything. You need the things you're already good at to be legible: written down clearly, structured so a machine can read it, and backed by enough credibility that the engine feels safe naming you.</p>

      <h3>{f.LFSH}</h3>
      <p>Most {f.SITES} carry a handful of service pages and a list of towns on the contact page. To a search engine — and to an AI answering a question about one specific {f.PLACE} — that reads as a {f.BIZ} rooted in one city that happens to travel.</p>
      <p>The {f.PRO} with a real page about {f.SILOEX}, with local markup and recent Google Business Profile activity, looks like the local option. Fixing it is unglamorous and effective: every service you sell, paired with every community you cover, plus the questions {f.CUSTS} actually ask around each one. {f.TOPICS} Done properly it lifts map pack rankings and gives AI engines something specific to cite at the same time.</p>

      <h3>Authority is the part most {f.PROS} skip</h3>
      <p>Good content on a site nobody vouches for tends to sit still. Historically the fix meant buying links, which works until it doesn't and leaves a mess behind. Building tiered links on Google's own properties is a different proposition — high trust, no marketplace footprint, and nothing you'd have to disavow if the rules tighten.</p>
      <p>Put the three together. Know where you stand. Publish content worth citing. Earn the credibility behind it. Do that and AI SEO stops being a buzzword. {f.LFCLOSE}</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

